From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] imx8mn-m7-echo: keep the resource table in DDR on silicon

On the i.MX 8M Nano the A53 cannot access the M7 TCMs while the M7 runs:
reads return stale data and writes are lost. imx_rproc copies the resource
table into the loaded image after the segments and later writes the vdev
status (DRIVER_OK) into it, so with the table in DTCM the firmware never
sees DRIVER_OK and never announces its rpmsg channel. QEMU does not model
this, which is why the QEMU build (DDR) works.

Move the TCM build's resource table to DDR at 0x550FF000, inside the
shared memory the firmware already maps uncached in its MPU. The device
tree reserves that page as imx_rproc's "rsc-table" memory region at the
same address, so Linux loads the table there and updates it in place.

Upstream-Status: Pending [RustAmp: move to memory-tcm.x on main]
Signed-off-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/examples/imx8mn-m7-echo/tcm.x
+++ b/examples/imx8mn-m7-echo/tcm.x
@@ -3,12 +3,19 @@
  * i.MX 8M Nano Cortex-M7, as seen from the M7. Linux (imx_rproc) loads the
  * ELF into the TCMs through its own aliases (ITCM at 0x007E0000, DTCM at
  * 0x00800000 on the A53 side). The M7 boots from the vector table at 0.
+ *
+ * The resource table is NOT in DTCM: while the M7 runs, the A53 cannot
+ * access the TCMs (reads return stale data, writes are lost), so Linux's
+ * vdev status write (DRIVER_OK) would never reach us. It lives in DDR at
+ * 0x550FF000 instead, inside the shared memory the MPU maps uncached, and
+ * the device tree reserves the same page as imx_rproc's "rsc-table" region,
+ * so Linux loads the table there and updates it in place.
  */
 MEMORY
 {
   ITCM : ORIGIN = 0x00000000, LENGTH = 128K   /* vectors, code, rodata, .data image */
   DTCM : ORIGIN = 0x20000000, LENGTH = 124K   /* data, bss, stack */
-  RSC  : ORIGIN = 0x2001F000, LENGTH = 4K     /* resource table */
+  RSC  : ORIGIN = 0x550FF000, LENGTH = 4K     /* resource table (DDR, shared) */
 }
 
 ENTRY(Reset)
